# Go toolchain: latest stable every 14 days

At least once every **14 days**, and whenever you touch Go toolchain files (`go.mod`, `go.work`, CI `setup-go`, Go Docker images), look up the **latest stable Go** from [go.dev/dl](https://go.dev/dl/) or the official release notes. Do **not** use tip, beta, or RC unless the user asks.

Record the last-checked date in the session. If that check is older than 14 days, check again **before** changing any `go` version pin.

Last checked: **2026-09-17T04:09Z** — latest stable **go1.27.1**. Exhaustive hunt on `origin/main` + this branch found no Go toolchain pins to bump.

## If this repo has Go

When `go.mod`, `**/*.go`, or Go CI already exists: update the `go` directive, keep `toolchain` consistent with that stable, pin CI `setup-go`, Docker `golang:` images, and docs to the same version, then run tests.

```
# go.mod — language line matches latest stable (1.27.1 on 2026-09-17)
go 1.27
toolchain go1.27.1
```

```yaml
# CI — pin setup-go to the same stable
- uses: actions/setup-go@v5
  with:
    go-version: "1.27.1"
```

## If this repo is not Go (Weasis is Java/OSGi)

Do **not** add Go sources, modules, or CI, and do **not** convert the app. The cadence still applies so agents remember to check; only steer Go modules/CI **if they already exist**.

Never copy unrelated method bodies. Don't invent preference keys. Don't `git add` Weasis anatomy DICOMs.
